 (Those of you who don't want to read my ranting just move on)

 (Nothing personal Brian, BTW)

I just read a comment in this newsgroup for about the nth time about
"does the dev team really read this group", and "why don't they do
such and so" and this and that.

Enough of that I tell you!  I'm kinda sick of it.

				~ * ~

First let me talk about the PC port of this game.

I've been working on/with 3.1 for about a 1.5 years now, and before that
I worked with 3.0 to get it released.  In all of that time I've only seen 2
people write in and express the desire to be among the people work on the
PC version of the game (neither of which have panned out).  As it stands now
there are about only 2 people who are doing *anything* to keep the game
going on the PC, and I'm not sure how much longer I'm going to want to do it.

Sure there's lots to try.  Gcc, djgcc, BC++, windows 3.0 port, but that's
not the only thing that needs to be done folks.  Not only that, but do
*you* know everything necessary to make each of those ports work?  Well
neither do I, but some of you out there expect *me* to learn, just so *you*
can play the game.  Ever think that sometimes new features that the developers
want to add break the game?  Most time is spent flushing out those, and
keeping makefiles up to date.  Not learning spiffy new compilers and 
windows coding.  And it all ain't done in just a night or two.  Your're
talking about a month of work to get any of these working properly.

386/486/XT! DOS 3.1-5.0! QEMM/386max! windows! C compilers!
You think I have them all??  You're crazy.

Yes.  I could use some help!  I've even posted here to r.g.h from time to
time asking for people to contribute (although not recently) and gotten
no response.  None.  Zip.  Zilch.  Zero.

Look.  You want this game to work on the PC?  Then maybe it's time to
learn something about a new product and the game itsself, and not just
wait for someone to do it for you.

In '89 I got involved because it seemed that there was no one out there willing
to step up and get this silly game running (full featured) on a PC.  Luckily
someone else (smarter than I) had managed to forge much of the path
(without my knowledge) just before I joined.  '89 was a long time ago,
he's gone now, and the game still goes on.

When I started I had never compiled a program on a PC in my life.  Now I'm
the one who's supporting the assembly code for the overlay manager, and
compiling the program weekly to test updates.  How?  I learned.  But I
certainly can't (and don't want to) learn it all.

				 ~ * ~

Now let's talk about what I know about the dev team.

*******************************************************************************
*> First, let me state once again, that I am NOT a dev team member.          <*
*> Nor do I speak for them in any way.  This is just my take on the subject. <*
*******************************************************************************

I just work on PC aspects of the game, and occasionally submit source code
changes to the dev team for their approval/inclusion.

Second, team members do read the group.  This I do know.  Maybe sometimes
they can't keep up with the content, and work on your game at the same time,
so what do you think they'll do.  Post ... or code?  That's all I'm going to
say about that.

Yes, I said "your game".  They certainly don't play it as much as the net
does and they make the game for you (not them) to play.  Why do you think
there's so many bugs?

Third, let's suppose that I've suddenly motivated you into wanting to
work on the PC version of the game.  

If you send a message to nethack-bugs saying, "let me join the PC team"
you will probably be politely told (by me) to do some coding and ask later.

Why?

The PC port doesn't need to support another hack from someone who felt like
working on the game this month.  Nor do I think it probable that you can
grok the 2.5 megs of source code and C structs to do any major coding right
the first time.

But ...

The dev team is looking for people with:
	commitment
	aptitude
	a few resources (ie can get mail and compile)
	and ideas.

Not just someone willing to send in a patch every once in a while.

You see, the dev team (as far as I understand) isn't looking to "take" the
game away from anyone, they're just trying to remove some of the chaos
inflicted upon the game via a general release of the source code to the net.
And the same holds true for all of the porting teams.

Besides, there's mailing lists you need to get on, coding utilities to find,
all kinds of people to meet, and stuff to learn just to get up to
speed.  Even a form of group discussion/source control.  It can't be done
for everyone.

				~ * ~

So, *if* (after all that) you're still interested, my advice is simple.  Play
the game, find a bug/feature, and fix/implement it (you might write in to see
if the bugs still needs fixing).  Then send in the diff.  Repeat until you're
a member of whatever NetHack team you wanted to join.  It's that simple.

So,  you think this is too aloof?  Perhaps.

                     too complex?  Probably.

Does it work?  You Bet.

Have I answered anybody's questions?  I don't care.

Do I feel better?  Vaguely.

				~ * ~

What do you get out of it?   Whatever kind of NetHack you've been asking for.

